{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_dbe6llrkgh6th6eprhkb4yh2tbgf7t22rd6dejx4ha4vsgopldfq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass, and I found and fixed one real divergence.\n\n## What the library is\n\n`c_src` is a single translation unit exporting exactly one symbol, `bin2hex` — libsodium's branch-free binary→hex encoder. Whole rejection surface: one `if (bin_len >= SIZE_MAX/2 || hex_maxlen <= bin_len*2U) abort();`, no null checks, no enums, no options.\n\n## Phase A artifacts (`translation/`)\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — `nm -D` inventory. C exports 1 strong symbol (`bin2hex`); Rust exports it too. 0 missing, 0 non-libc undefined. No C module was skipped (CMake compiles only `src/lib.c`), so nothing needed translating.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 16 rows, grepped from the source: both `||` branches incl. short-circuit ordering, the `<=` (not `<`) boundary, `SIZE_MAX/2` boundaries, NULL-deref `SIGSEGV` rows, and one-step-inside-range non-rejection. Documents *why* the out-of-range-enum class is absent (no enum params) and maps it to out-of-range lengths.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 28 rows over the axes the C actually branches on (nibble digit-vs-letter correction paths, byte packing order, `hex_maxlen` slack, buffer aliasing/overlap, unalignment, guard-page exact fit).\n\n## Phases B–D\n\nTests load **both** `.so`s via `libloading` and call only exported symbols. 28 valid-path tests (exhaustive over all 256 bytes and all 65 536 byte pairs, plus fixed-seed randomized sweeps), 18 error-path tests (each crashing case in a `fork()`ed child comparing exact signal), 4 symbol-parity tests. Run across all feature combos × dev/release via `tests/feature_matrix.sh`: **24/24 test-binary runs green, 0 failures.**\n\n## The bug found and fixed\n\n`src/lib.rs` used place-expression derefs (`*bin.add(i)`, `*hex.add(k) = v`). In any build with `debug-assertions` (the default `dev` profile) rustc injects a null-pointer assertion, so a NULL `hex`/`bin` died with **`SIGABRT`** (\"null pointer dereference occurred\") where C dies with **`SIGSEGV`**. Release builds hid this entirely. Switched to `core::ptr::read`/`write` with `wrapping_add` addressing — plain load/store, no injected checks, identical failure mode under every profile.\n\nTwo supporting fixes: the harness was silently falling back to the release `.so` during \"dev\" runs (`cargo test` doesn't build a `cdylib`) — now profile-strict and it builds each profile explicitly; and `PR_SET_DUMPABLE(0)` in the fork child cut the suite from 102 s to 7.5 s while *widening* coverage, since `systemd-coredump` was charging ~65 ms per fatal signal.\n\nTwo deliberate mutations (swapped nibble packing; `<=`→`<`) were both caught, confirming the suite isn't vacuous. `c_src/` is unmodified apart from the `build/` directory the task asked for."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":879,"cache_read_input_tokens":126924,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":879,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"c60e702c-63fb-4adc-a7d2-070106c8e88a","uuid":"f8c72929-1da6-406a-b4eb-f1bd6d74276d","timestamp":"2026-08-26T20:08:30.936Z"}
